AB  - In this chapter, we reviewed the use of large-scale genomic data from individuals and populations in tree population genomics, which, along with recent advances in sequencing methods and bioinformatics, has transformed research in forest sciences. Population genomics is a cornerstone for the investigation of the molecular bases of tree fitness under different ecological and climatic conditions, local adaptation, and speciation. Together with traditional population genetic methods, genomics offers innovative methods and a comprehensive genome-wide approach for studying and understanding adaptive evolutionary processes such as mutation, genetic drift, gene flow, and inbreeding, as well as the impact of genetic intraspecific variation in tree species, by providing information on multiple locus-specific effects and identifying functional genomic elements. In addition, epigenome-wide data at population scales possess a great potential to study phenotypic tree plasticity under different environmental scenarios, with special interest in the identification of genomic epivariants that can be linked with local adaptation and responses to biotic and abiotic stress. The use of genome-wide data has widely contributed to phylogenomics, allowing us to infer individual genetic admixtures and more accurately reconstruct phylogenetic trees. Finally, we addressed the application of tree population genomics for modern tree breeding and conservation programs, improving their efficiency and our understanding of the genetic impacts of management activities. This knowledge is crucial for the conservation and sustainable management of forest genetic resources. © 2025 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
