TI  - Progress and Strategies for Conservation and Utilisation of Coffea canephora Genetic Diversity for Variety Development
PY  - 2026
T2  - Coffee Science and Biotechnology: Genomic Biochemical and Nanotechnological Perspectives
SP  - 38
EP  - 65
DO  - 10.1201/9781003689393-2
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045027895?origin=resultslist
AD  - Department of Crop Science and Horticulture, Makerere University, Kampala, Uganda
AD  - National Coffee Research Institute, National Agricultural Research Organisation, Kituza-Mukono, Uganda
AD  - Plant Genetic Resource Center, National Agricultural Research Organisation, Entebbe, Uganda
AD  - Faculty of Agricultural Sciences, Uganda Christian University, Mukono, Uganda
AD  - Laboratory of Plant Breeding & Biometry, Department of Crop Science, Agricultural University of Athens, Iera Odos 75, Athens, 11855, Greece
AB  - This chapter presents an integrated synthesis of the genetic diversity, conservation, and cultivation of Coffea canephora (Robusta coffee), with a particular emphasis on Uganda’s unique contributions to global coffee sustainability. It begins with an exploration of the taxonomy, origin, and evolutionary relationships within the genus Coffea, underscoring the importance of wild and underutilised species in climate resilience, including resistance to abiotic and biotic stressors. The geographic distribution and centres of diversity are contextualised through Africa’s biodiversity hotspots, with Uganda highlighted as a key reservoir for Robusta germplasm. The chapter traces historical developments in Uganda’s coffee sector, from colonial introductions and varietal selection to liberalisation and the rise of smallholder production. It critically examines the impacts of land tenure insecurity, ecological degradation, and shifting cultivation zones on production efficiency and biodiversity conservation. Studies on genetic structure spanning phenotypic characterisation, early isozyme analyses, microsatellite markers, and recent genome-wide approaches reveal regional adaptation patterns and the emergence of unique subgroups such as the Ugandan “Sub-group O.” A focus is placed on conservation strategies encompassing ex situ and in situ efforts, germplasm documentation, field collection constraints, and community-based stewardship. Methodological challenges related to germplasm sampling, hybridisation, and administrative procedures are dissected to clarify their implications for breeding and genetic representativeness. Modern tools such as landscape genomics, species distribution modelling, and predictive analytics are discussed in relation to resilience breeding and habitat planning. Drawing on global perspectives, the chapter emphasises the need for coordinated international networks, participatory breeding models, and technological innovations, including bioinformatics and Artificial Intelligence, to safeguard coffee’s genetic heritage. It concludes by identifying knowledge gaps in documentation, adaptive trait profiling, and farmer adoption, and offers strategic recommendations to strengthen Uganda’s coffee sector through inclusive, science-based interventions. Together, these insights contribute to the sustainable management of Coffea canephora under changing climatic and socio-economic conditions. Additionally, strengthening national germplasm repositories, enhancing farmers’ access to diverse planting materials, and integrating genetic conservation into agricultural development frameworks are critical steps toward preserving Uganda’s role as a global reference point for Robusta coffee diversity and innovation. © 2027 Erdi Can Aytar.
